{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018PV1etvDYCA3fX6rsZivsK","type":"tool_result","content":"  1401 draft-v1.md\n   883 outline.md\n  2284 total\n2:## *\"Frozen, superseded, and inert without a controller\"*\n10:## Attention Budget\n46:## 🧭 Soundings\n97:## Why This Chapter Matters\n119:## What You'll Learn\n134:## ⚪ §1 — Where LoadBalancer Runs Out\n142:### The layer boundary\n150:### North-south and east-west\n160:### The edge router\n170:### What comes next, and the first thing worth knowing about it\n180:## 🔵 §2 — Routing by Host and Path\n186:### What Ingress is\n194:### And what it refuses to do\n204:### The shapes an Ingress takes\n354:### The distinction Chapter 9 asked for\n364:### The fields, briefly\n392:## ⚪ §3 — The Object Is Not the Implementation\n402:### What an Ingress controller is\n408:### The rule, retrieved\n425:### Naming which controller: IngressClass\n446:### The honest note\n464:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1\n468:**1.** ⚪ `[retrieval: ch9]` You need to expose a PostgreSQL database and a web application to clients outside the cluster. Which one can an Ingress handle, and what do you use for the other?\n470:**2.** ⚪ One IP address serves `shop.example.com` and `blog.example.com`, routing each to a different Service. Name the Ingress capability. Then: one host, with `/catalog` and `/checkout` going to different Services. Name that one.\n472:**3.** 🔵 A colleague applies a correct Ingress manifest to a fresh cluster. `kubectl get ingress` shows it. No traffic reaches the application. Name the most likely cause, and say what `kubectl get` actually proves.\n474:**4.** 🔵 The same Ingress manifest is applied to two clusters, each running a different Ingress controller. Should you expect identical behaviour? Justify your answer.\n476:**5.** 🔵 `[retrieval: ch3]` Chapter 3 gave you a one-sentence rule about objects and components. State it, then name the two things from Chapter 9 that were instances of it.\n482:**1. The web application, over HTTP/HTTPS. The database needs `Service.Type=NodePort` or `Service.Type=LoadBalancer`.**\n490:**2. Name-based virtual hosting; simple fanout.**\n494:**3. No Ingress controller is installed. `kubectl get` proves only that the object exists.**\n500:**4. No.** Ideally all Ingress controllers should fit the reference specification; in reality, the various Ingress controllers operate slightly differently [source: k8s-docs-ingress-depth-2026-08-24].\n504:**5. `[retrieval: ch3]` An object without its component does nothing.** The two Chapter 9 instances: a `type: LoadBalancer` Service on a cluster with no load balancer to provision one, and a Service whose selector matches no Pods.\n521:## ⚪ §4 — Frozen, Not Deprecated\n525:### The statement\n540:### The split\n551:### Why \"deprecated\" is a different word\n565:### What \"recommends\" obliges\n579:## 🟡 §5 — Roles, Not Just Routes\n583:### The idea\n589:### The three roles\n599:### The resources\n614:### The mapping, which is the whole design\n697:### The request flow\n712:### The other design principles\n720:### Is it there?\n733:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2\n737:**1.** ⚪ State both halves of what the Kubernetes project has said about the Ingress API.\n739:**2.** ⚪ True or false, with justification: *Ingress is deprecated and will be removed in a future release.*\n741:**3.** 🟡 Name the three role-mapped Gateway API resources and say which organisational role each belongs to.\n743:**4.** 🟡 How many GatewayClasses is a Gateway associated with, and how many Routes can attach to one Gateway?\n745:**5.** 🟡 A request arrives at a Gateway's IP address. Name the header the reverse proxy uses to match a configuration, and name the two optional things the HTTPRoute may do before the request is forwarded.\n751:**1. It is frozen: generally available and subject to the stability guarantees for GA APIs, with no plans for removal — *and* no longer being developed, with no further changes or updates** [source: k8s-docs-ingress-depth-2026-08-24].\n757:**2. False, on both counts.** Ingress has not been deprecated, and there are no plans to remove it [source: k8s-docs-ingress-depth-2026-08-24]. What has been said is that it will not be developed further, and that the project recommends Gateway for new work.\n761:**3. GatewayClass — infrastructure provider. Gateway — cluster operator. HTTPRoute — application developer** [source: k8s-docs-gateway-api-depth-2026-08-24].\n767:**4. Exactly one GatewayClass. Many Routes** [source: k8s-docs-gateway-api-depth-2026-08-24].\n771:**5. The `Host:` header. Then, optionally: header and/or path matching based on the HTTPRoute's match rules, and optional modification of the request based on its filter rules** [source: k8s-docs-gateway-api-depth-2026-08-24].\n787:## 🔵 §6 — Allowing, Never Denying\n795:### What a NetworkPolicy controls\n803:### Three identifiers, and two selectors doing different jobs\n860:### The two sorts of isolation\n878:### Additive, and there is no deny\n913:### Both ends must allow it\n925:### Getting default-deny with no deny rule\n951:### The two exceptions\n969:## 🟡 §7 — What NetworkPolicy Cannot Do\n973:### The prerequisite\n981:### Why this one is worse\n995:### Where else could it possibly live?\n1005:### What it cannot do, stated flat\n1038:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #3\n1042:**1.** 🔵 `[retrieval: ch4]` Chapter 4 said a NetworkPolicy selects both its subject and its peers. Point at the two selectors in that sentence and say what each one is choosing.\n1044:**2.** 🔵 A Pod in namespace `prod` has no NetworkPolicy selecting it anywhere in the cluster. What inbound and outbound traffic is permitted?\n1046:**3.** 🟡 **⚠️ This one is intentionally hard. Struggle is the point.** Two NetworkPolicies select the same Pod. Policy A permits inbound traffic from `app: web`. Policy B permits inbound traffic from `app: batch`. What is permitted — and could a third policy be written to forbid `app: web`?\n1048:**4.** 🟡 Pod `frontend` has an egress policy permitting traffic to `app: api`. Pod `api` has an ingress policy permitting traffic only from `app: admin`. Can `frontend` reach `api`?\n1050:**5.** 🔵 You apply a NetworkPolicy intended to block traffic from a specific Pod. Traffic still flows. Name two distinct explanations, one of which is not a mistake in the policy.\n1056:**1. `[retrieval: ch4]` The policy's own top-level `podSelector` chooses which Pods the policy applies to. The selectors inside its rules — `podSelector` and `namespaceSelector` under `from`/`to` — choose which Pods and namespaces those Pods may talk to.**\n1062:**2. All of both — the Pod is non-isolated for ingress and for egress.**\n1068:**3. Inbound from both `app: web` and `app: batch` — the lists combine additively. And no: there is no deny rule, so nothing can subtract a permission. Removing that access means removing the grant.**\n1076:**4. No.** For a connection from a source Pod to a destination Pod to be allowed, both the egress policy on the source and the ingress policy on the destination must allow it; if either side does not, the connection will not happen [source: k8s-docs-network-policies-depth-2026-08-24].\n1080:**5. Either: the network plugin does not implement NetworkPolicy, so the resource has no effect at all; or the traffic falls under one of the unconditional exceptions — a Pod cannot block access to itself, and traffic to and from its own node is always allowed** [source: k8s-docs-network-policies-depth-2026-08-24].","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"406e0880-28ff-45b2-9e68-5356bbbaa38c","uuid":"016a4b2e-7a77-40a4-a6ca-9a8203ff78ea","timestamp":"2026-08-25T04:15:31.698Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"  1401 draft-v1.md\n   883 outline.md\n  2284 total\n2:## *\"Frozen, superseded, and inert without a controller\"*\n10:## Attention Budget\n46:## 🧭 Soundings\n97:## Why This Chapter Matters\n119:## What You'll Learn\n134:## ⚪ §1 — Where LoadBalancer Runs Out\n142:### The lay