{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015YUWYBMvGtsG21Jze2Pytx","type":"tool_result","content":"468:**1.** ⚪ `[retrieval: ch9]` You need to expose a PostgreSQL database and a web application to clients outside the cluster. Which one can an Ingress handle, and what do you use for the other?\n476:**5.** 🔵 `[retrieval: ch3]` Chapter 3 gave you a one-sentence rule about objects and components. State it, then name the two things from Chapter 9 that were instances of it.\n504:**5. `[retrieval: ch3]` An object without its component does nothing.** The two Chapter 9 instances: a `type: LoadBalancer` Service on a cluster with no load balancer to provision one, and a Service whose selector matches no Pods.\n1042:**1.** 🔵 `[retrieval: ch4]` Chapter 4 said a NetworkPolicy selects both its subject and its peers. Point at the two selectors in that sentence and say what each one is choosing.\n1056:**1. `[retrieval: ch4]` The policy's own top-level `podSelector` chooses which Pods the policy applies to. The selectors inside its rules — `podSelector` and `namespaceSelector` under `from`/`to` — choose which Pods and namespaces those Pods may talk to.**\n1213:**2.** 🔵 `[retrieval: ch9]` You need to expose an HTTP application and a message broker speaking its own binary protocol, both to clients outside the cluster. How many Ingresses do you need, and how many Services of which types?\n1223:**7.** 🔵 `[retrieval: ch3]` Chapter 3 said a controller watches for objects and drives reality toward what they describe. An Ingress controller is one. Name what it watches and name what it changes.\n1243:**17.** 🟡 `[retrieval: ch3]` Name the rule Chapter 3 gave you about objects and components, then list every instance of it you have met in this book so far.\n1255:**2. `[retrieval: ch9]` One Ingress, for the HTTP application. The broker goes to a Service of type NodePort or LoadBalancer — an Ingress cannot carry it.**\n1281:**7. `[retrieval: ch3]` It watches Ingress objects, and the Services those objects reference. It changes a load balancer — or the edge router, or additional frontends — to match.**\n1341:**17. `[retrieval: ch3]` An object without its component does nothing.**","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"406e0880-28ff-45b2-9e68-5356bbbaa38c","uuid":"8af331fe-9ae1-46a6-b9ae-e5dc1b4bf6fb","timestamp":"2026-08-25T04:20:27.982Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"468:**1.** ⚪ `[retrieval: ch9]` You need to expose a PostgreSQL database and a web application to clients outside the cluster. Which one can an Ingress handle, and what do you use for the other?\n476:**5.** 🔵 `[retrieval: ch3]` Chapter 3 gave you a one-sentence rule about objects and components. State it, then name the two things from Chapter 9 that were instances of it.\n504:**5. `[retrieval: ch3]` An object without its component does nothing.** The two Chapter 9 instances: a `type: LoadBalancer` Service on a cluster with no load balancer to provision one, and a Service whose selector matches no Pods.\n1042:**1.** 🔵 `[retrieval: ch4]` Chapter 4 said a NetworkPolicy selects both its subject and its peers. Point at the two selectors in that sentence and say what each one is choosing.\n1056:**1. `[retrieval: ch4]` The policy's own top-level `podSelector` chooses which Pods the policy applies to. The selectors inside its rules — `podSelector` and `namespaceSelector` under `from`/`to` — choose which Pods and namespaces those Pods may talk to.**\n1213:**2.** 🔵 `[retrieval: ch9]` You need to expose an HTTP application and a message broker speaking its own binary protocol, both to clients outside the cluster. How many Ingresses do you need, and how many Services of which types?\n1223:**7.** 🔵 `[retrieval: ch3]` Chapter 3 said a controller watches for objects and drives reality toward what they describe. An Ingress controller is one. Name what it watches and name what it changes.\n1243:**17.** 🟡 `[retrieval: ch3]` Name the rule Chapter 3 gave you about objects and components, then list every instance of it you have met in this book so far.\n1255:**2. `[retrieval: ch9]` One Ingress, for the HTTP application. The broker goes to a Service of type NodePort or LoadBalancer — an Ingress cannot carry it.**\n1281:**7. `[retrieval: ch3]` It watches Ingress objects, and the Services those objects reference. It changes a load balancer — or the edge router, or additional frontends — to match.**\n1341:**17. `[retrieval: ch3]` An object without its component does nothing.**","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
